# Kanji Hunt — PRD

**Version:** v0.1 draft, 25 Nov 2026 · **Owner:** me · **Next revision:** Sun 29 Nov, with Phase 1 data

## Problem
Tokyo surrounds a beginner learner with kanji they see every day but can't read. Google Lens helps you read a sign once. Nothing makes sure you can read it the next time you pass it. List-based SRS apps teach words disconnected from where you actually met them.

## User
Primary user: me. I'm a beginner learner who commutes daily on the Tokyo Metro **Tozai** and **Yurakucho** lines. Both run mostly underground, so signal is patchy. Trains are often crush-loaded: one hand is on a strap, there's no audio, and I get 30–90 seconds between stations.

## Core loop
1. Photograph kanji in a public space (station sign, menu, notice).
2. Crop the word on the device.
3. Send only the crop to the AI, which returns the text, reading and meaning.
4. Check the result against JMdict (and JMnedict for place and station names).
5. Create a card with the full photo, which never leaves the phone, as a memory hook. Mark it **verified**, **flagged** (AI and dictionary disagree) or **not found**.
6. Show the catch reply, e.g. "New catch! 駅 — 48 of 2,136". The count is the new Jōyō kanji contained in the caught word.
7. Review in a one-thumb FSRS swipe feed that works offline. Sessions are capped, and there is no backlog counter.

## v1 scope (ship by Sun 27 Dec)
- Swipe feed with FSRS scheduling, as an offline PWA.
- Camera capture and on-device crop.
- AI call through one serverless function, so the API key stays off the client.
- Dictionary verification with visible flags.
- Catch reply.
- `eval/` script, plus error rates published in the README.
- Local usage log (sessions and dates).

## Non-goals for v1
- **Deferred to v2:** collection page, rarity, location sets, map.
- **Out of scope:** WhatsApp/LINE, accounts or sync, audio, handwriting, grammar, cloud photo storage, native apps.

## Success metrics
**Usage**
- Used on 10+ commutes by 27 Dec, measured from the usage log.
- Median session length of 30–90 s.

**Accuracy** (provisional, to be revised 29 Nov)
- A card is *correct* when the selected word, its reading in context and its core meaning are all right.
- At least 80% of test photos correct end to end (n ≥ 30 by 27 Dec).
- At least 50% of incorrect cards are flagged.
- Error rate on verified cards is published.

**Checkpoint (27 Dec):** continue if at least 2 of 3 are true:
- used on 10+ commutes;
- most test photos are correct;
- I'm still interested.

## Constraints
- **Time:** about 7 h/week, never below 3, own time and laptop only.
- **Cost:** free tiers only.
- **Privacy:** photos stay on the device. Only crops of public text are sent to the AI. No work or client material, ever.
- **Licences:** JMdict, JMnedict and KANJIDIC are EDRDG data under CC BY-SA. Attribution goes in the app and the README.

## Stack
- **App:** Vite + plain JS PWA, Dexie (IndexedDB), ts-fsrs.
- **Backend:** Cloudflare Pages and one Worker.
- **AI and dictionary:** Gemini Flash free tier; trimmed JMdict in D1.
- **Eval:** Python + DuckDB.

## Risks and mitigations
| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Scene OCR fails on vertical or stylised text, glare and motion blur | Tight on-device crop; compare engines on real photos |
| Station names have irregular readings (高田馬場 = たかだのばば) and aren't in JMdict, so they get falsely flagged | Add JMnedict and a Tokyo Metro station list |
| Free-tier quota or data-use terms change | Send crops only; the provider sits behind one Worker function, so it can be swapped |
| I over-plan instead of building | This PRD stays one page; it changes only when new data comes in |

